ertainly people who hadn't  voted for a long while  who felt Jeremy was speaking to and for  them and the Labour was speaking to and  for them and I don't think any of us can  argue with that and therefore I think  he's earned the right to try and get  labour into power and earn the right to  be our next Prime Minister now one of  the things that you were perhaps most  passionate about during that leadership  I can't  was breakfast and the UK's relationship  with the EU I just want to read to you a  couple of things that you said in March  my view is absolutely crystal clear that  we should be members of the single  market and then you said when we leave  the single market we will have a lesser  level of benefits than we currently  enjoy but when you have a chance to vote  in favour of staying in a single market  this week you abstained  so did you sell out your principles for  a front bench job no I think my view  hasn't changed I still believe that  brexit is a is a bad decision for us as  a country and is going to leave us worse  off that's my personal view but jeremy  corbyn's asked me to do a very important  job on the front bench for labour in  Northern Ireland at a point at which  Northern Ireland is central to British  politics and therefore I think my duty  is to get on and try and do that job and  to work as part of it as I say a Labour  government in Waiting at the same time  there you have had some pretty strong  things about that single market  membership in the past so I do just want  to stay on this for a moment  because you said that it was the biggest  gut FIB that the government is telling  right now that we're going to be able to  enjoy the exact same benefits outside  the single market as we currently enjoy  I mean that is Labour's position now so  why is it a fib when the government says  it and not a feared one neighbor says it  well at my view Sophie is that we are  going to find out over the next couple  of months that the government can't  d